    ("In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould had killed bacteria on one of his culture dishes at St Mary's Hospital in London. He identified the mould as Penicillium and named the active substance penicillin, but he struggled to purify it. It was not until 1940 that Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, working at Oxford, turned it into a usable drug. Mass production during the Second World War saved thousands of soldiers from infected wounds.",
     S1, ["Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould killed bacteria", "Florey and Chain made it a usable drug from 1940", "mass production in WWII saved soldiers"], [["1928", "Fleming"], ["Florey", "Chain", "1940"], ["war", "soldiers"]], E),
    ("The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of trade routes linking China with the Mediterranean from about the second century BCE. Merchants rarely travelled the whole distance; goods such as silk, spices and paper passed through many hands. The network also carried ideas and religions, including Buddhism, and diseases such as the plague. It declined as sea routes became cheaper and as the Ottoman Empire controlled key territories in the fifteenth century.",
     S1, ["network of trade routes linking China and the Mediterranean", "carried goods, ideas and diseases", "declined as sea routes grew cheaper / Ottoman control"], [["network", "routes"], ["ideas", "religion", "Buddhism", "disease"], ["sea", "Ottoman"]], E),
    ("Honeybees communicate the location of food through a waggle dance. A forager that finds nectar returns to the hive and dances on the comb: the angle of the dance relative to vertical shows the direction of the food relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle shows the distance. Other bees follow the dancer and fly out to the flowers. The behaviour, decoded by Karl von Frisch in the 1940s, earned him a Nobel Prize in 1973.",
     S1, ["bees use a waggle dance to signal food", "angle shows direction, duration shows distance", "decoded by Karl von Frisch, Nobel Prize 1973"], [["waggle"], ["angle", "direction", "distance"], ["von Frisch"]], E),
    ("The Panama Canal opened in 1914 and shortened the sea journey between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans by about 8,000 miles. It uses a system of locks to lift ships 26 metres above sea level to Gatun Lake. An expansion completed in 2016 added a third set of locks that allows much larger vessels, called neopanamax ships, to pass. About 14,000 vessels use the canal each year, and drought in 2023 forced authorities to reduce daily crossings.",
     ("max_words", 35), ["canal opened in 1914, saving about 8,000 miles", "2016 expansion added larger locks", "2023 drought reduced crossings"], [["1914"], ["2016", "expansion", "third"], ["drought"]], E),
